:name: EX-S07 - a retirement in your name
:description: Your own SPI retired an SA. Did you send it - or does someone else hold your key?

# Execution profile (design 4.3; rules G1/G2 of section 9.2). FIRST, so a profile that cannot be
# found takes the machines down loudly instead of leaving every node running with no quantum set.
$profile?=@~/space-cs-sim/scripts/profiles/interactive.resc
include $profile

# Every path hangs off $out, which has NO default - see any other scenario in this repository for
# why. Unset, LoadELF fails where you can see it.
#
#   renode -e '$out=@'"$(make -s out)" -e 'include @<this file>' -e start
$comm?=$out/build-comm-s06-hard/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$obc?=$out/build-obc-u02-hard/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$eps?=$out/build-eps-hard/zephyr/zephyr.elf

# THERE IS NO ATTACKER MACHINE IN THIS SCENARIO. Every party is a host-side ground station on the
# SPACE LINK, as in EX-S04 to EX-S06: the operator's station, and - in the runs that need one - a
# second station holding a copy of the operator's SPI 10 key, or the partner holding its own SPI 11.
# They are separate station objects, because the whole of EX-S07 is that the operator's own record
# of what IT sent is different from what the spacecraft heard under its key.
#
# The firmware is EX-S06's HARDENED image in every run - EX-S07 needs the SA-management log - and
# there is no vulnerable/mitigated image pair. This is a host-side exercise, like EX-G01: the one
# difference is whether the operator's station reconciles the log against its own ledger.
$comm1?=$out/build-comm-s01-sat1/zephyr/zephyr.elf

$comm0uart?=$out/s07-sat0-comm.uart
$obc0uart?=$out/s07-sat0-obc.uart
$eps0uart?=$out/s07-sat0-eps.uart
$comm1uart?=$out/s07-sat1-comm.uart
$linkport?=3777

emulation CreateCANHub "canHub0"
emulation CreateCANHub "canHub1"
emulation CreateCANHub "crosslinkHub"

# --- satellite 0: the victim. Everything hardened. ------------------------------------------------

mach create "SAT0_COMM"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub0
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan2 crosslinkHub
sysbus LoadELF $comm
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $comm0uart true
emulation CreateServerSocketTerminal $linkport "spacelink0" false
connector Connect sysbus.usart2 spacelink0
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1
logLevel 3 fdcan2

mach create "SAT0_OBC"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub0
sysbus LoadELF $obc
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $obc0uart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

mach create "SAT0_EPS"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub0
sysbus LoadELF $eps
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $eps0uart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

# --- satellite 1: the neighbour, so the crosslink has a far end. ----------------------------------
#
# Present for the same reason it is in EX-S03, EX-S05, EX-L03 and EX-U01: this COMM image is built
# with the crosslink on, and a crosslink attached to nothing is a node logging a failure at startup
# for a reason that has nothing to do with the exercise. EX-S06's retirements are on the space link.

mach create "SAT1_COMM"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub1
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan2 crosslinkHub
sysbus LoadELF $comm1
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $comm1uart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1
logLevel 3 fdcan2
